<Docs
	page="recipes/gleam"
	title="Gleam"
	lede="sqlight and pog work out of the box; squirrel's queries are plain SQL files."
>
	<section>
		<h2>Which strings squill rewrites</h2>
		<p>
			Every Gleam string takes raw newlines, so any string the query finds is
			fair game, one line or many: squill puts the quotes on their own lines and
			lays the query out one clause per line.
		</p>
		<p>
			The built-in <code>gleam</code> grammar&rsquo;s default query looks for a
			string passed straight to a <code>query</code>, <code>exec</code>, or{" "}
			<code>execute</code> call, module-qualified (<code>sqlight.query</code>)
			or imported bare, or piped into one (
			<code>"&hellip;" |&gt; pog.query</code>). A string bound to a variable
			first (<code>let sql = "&hellip;"</code>, then <code>pog.query(sql)</code>
			) is out of its sight.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>sqlight</h2>
		<p>
			sqlight is SQLite, and the default query knows it:{" "}
			<code>sqlight.query</code> and <code>sqlight.exec</code> format as SQLite
			whatever the configured dialect. (Imported bare, the calls can&rsquo;t be
			told apart from pog&rsquo;s, and use the configured dialect &mdash; set{" "}
			<code>dialect = "sqlite"</code> in the rule.)
		</p>
		<Recipe name="gleam-sqlight" lang="gleam" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>pog</h2>
		<p>
			pog&rsquo;s calls use the configured dialect, Postgres unless you say
			otherwise. The string can be passed in or piped in (
			<code>"&hellip;" |&gt; pog.query</code>); the default query finds both:
		</p>
		<Recipe name="gleam-pog" lang="gleam" />
	</section>

	<section>
		<h2>squirrel</h2>
		<p>
			squirrel keeps each query in its own <code>.sql</code> file under a{" "}
			<code>sql/</code> directory, and <code>.sql</code> files format with no
			rule at all. The comment at the top of a file becomes the generated
			function&rsquo;s documentation, and squill never moves or drops a comment.
			The one thing to configure is the <code>sql.gleam</code> module squirrel
			generates from them: it&rsquo;s squirrel&rsquo;s output, so leave it to
			squirrel.
		</p>
		<Recipe name="gleam-squirrel" lang="sql" />
		<p>
			After formatting, run <code>gleam run -m squirrel</code> again: the
			generated code carries a copy of each query&rsquo;s text.
		</p>
	</section>
</Docs>
